WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.120
This next section of the course will deal with&nbsp;
aggregates, summaries of numeric portions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.720
our data. There are several aggregates to cover,&nbsp;
but I will start with what is, for most, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:19.920
common one, and that is SUM. Take a look at this&nbsp;
query. It just has each salesperson by name and ID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.440
and the total for each salesperson.&nbsp;
I have joined the tables necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.440
get the names of the salespersons. I will&nbsp;
execute this query, and we see right away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.840
a very common problem people see when&nbsp;
trying to run a query with aggregates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.280
such as this SUM aggregate we see. The error&nbsp;
message states that the LastName column is invalid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.800
because it is not being used in an aggregate,&nbsp;
which is true, it is not being summarized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:57.120
and there is no GROUP BY clause. For aggregates&nbsp;
in SQL, this is the important part: The query has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.360
to be told how to summarize the data. In this&nbsp;
case, is it just for each SalesPersonID? Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.600
just for each last name? In reality, any column&nbsp;
not in an aggregate in a query like this needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:17.920
be grouped. Thus, we need to add a GROUP BY clause&nbsp;
so that this query is grouped by the three columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.240
above the summary. Here is a real-life trick&nbsp;
I like to use. Notice that I have the columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.880
listed first and then the SUM aggregate. Doing&nbsp;
it this way makes it easy for me to add the words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:36.160
GROUP BY and then copy and paste the columns&nbsp;
to be grouped here. I will execute this again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.280
and we see the SUM total of&nbsp;
sales for each salesperson.

